1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:10,080
It all started in 1951, when my father
enabled me to go to a programming

2
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:13,069
course in Cambridge, England.

3
00:00:13,999 --> 00:00:17,911
It was a frightening experience: it
was the first time that I left the

4
00:00:17,911 --> 00:00:23,160
Netherlands, the first time I ever had
to understand people speaking English and

5
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:35,500
immediately I was all by myself, trying
to follow a course on a totally new topic.

6
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:35,619
But I liked it very much.

7
00:00:35,619 --> 00:00:40,690
The Netherlands was such a small country
that Aad van Wijngaarden, who was the

8
00:00:40,690 --> 00:00:50,750
director of the Computation Department of
the Mathematical Centre in Amsterdam, knew

9
00:00:50,750 --> 00:01:01,630
of this, and he offered me a job, which
I could only accept in March of 1952.

10
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:06,188
And on a part-time basis, I became the
programmer of the Mathematical Centre.

11
00:01:06,188 --> 00:01:10,770
They didn’t have computers yet;
they were trying to build them.

12
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:28,020
I programmed until 1962 and the first
eight years of my programming there

13
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:32,720
I developed the basic software for a
series of successive machines being

14
00:01:32,730 --> 00:01:33,980
built at the Mathematical Centre.

15
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:34,014
And in those beginning years I was
a very conservative programmer.

16
00:01:34,014 --> 00:01:34,020
The way in which programs were written
down, the form of the instruction code on

17
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:34,026
paper and the library organization, input,
output, etc., it was very much modeled

18
00:01:34,026 --> 00:02:18,140
after what I had seen in ’51 in Cambridge,
but it’s all worked satisfactorily.

19
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:23,700
It is my understanding that when you got
married, you could not enter the term

20
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:27,839
“programmer” into your marriage record?

21
00:02:27,839 --> 00:02:27,999
That’s true.

22
00:02:28,070 --> 00:02:28,870
Yes.

23
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,880
When could that be recognized as a title?

24
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:38,440
I think in the early sixties.

25
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:43,770
I mean it’s a well-known and true story.

26
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:50,940
I was supposed to study theoretical
physics, I did so and that was the

27
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:57,949
reason for going to Cambridge, because
I was being groomed to become an

28
00:02:57,960 --> 00:02:59,750
excellent theoretical physicist.

29
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:06,740
The idea was that if besides all the
usual tools I could also use computers, I

30
00:03:06,790 --> 00:03:13,219
could perhaps become a better theoretical
physicist, be more than average.

31
00:03:15,039 --> 00:03:22,540
However, in ’55 after three years of
programming, while I was still a student,

32
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:35,469
I concluded that the intellectual
challenge of programming was greater

33
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:41,380
than the intellectual challenge of
theoretical physics, and as a result

34
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:53,910
I chose programming, the reason being
that programming was so unforgiving.

35
00:03:55,630 --> 00:04:02,270
If something went wrong, I mean a
zero is a zero and a one is a one.

36
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:06,510
I had never used someone else’s software.

37
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:12,370
I used machines that I had myself
been involved in the design of,

38
00:04:13,090 --> 00:04:18,340
I had documented the instruction
code and I had written my own basic

39
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:22,629
software and all the programs and if
something went wrong I had done it.

40
00:04:23,339 --> 00:04:28,910
And it was that unforgiving-ness
that challenged me, because I

41
00:04:28,910 --> 00:04:42,370
also began to realize that though
in principle it’s very simple.

42
00:04:43,560 --> 00:04:46,710
A zero is a zero and a one is a one, and
you should have complete intellectual

43
00:04:46,779 --> 00:04:58,155
control of what you are doing, in some
strange and not understood way, programs

44
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:02,590
could become very complicated, tricky.

45
00:05:05,289 --> 00:05:12,870
So it was in ’55 when I decided
not to become a physicist, to

46
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:12,885
become a programmer instead.

47
00:05:12,890 --> 00:05:19,909
It was a difficult decision because,
as I said, I was groomed to become

48
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:26,169
a first-class scientist, and at the
time programming didn’t look like

49
00:05:26,169 --> 00:05:38,940
doing science, it was just a mixture
of being ingenious and being accurate.

50
00:05:40,669 --> 00:05:49,760
I remember that I envied my hardware
friends, because if you asked them what

51
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:55,480
their professional competence consisted
of, they could point out that they knew

52
00:05:56,100 --> 00:06:02,676
everything about triodes, pentodes,
the valves and other electronic gear.

53
00:06:02,690 --> 00:06:04,150
And there was nothing I could point to!

54
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:09,614
Actually, well, I had made a number of
programs and I had a very good handwriting

55
00:06:09,614 --> 00:06:18,420
So, how did you go about making
computing science respectable then?

56
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:24,490
Well, that happened later.

57
00:06:28,929 --> 00:06:33,080
I spoke with van Wijngaarden in
’55, and explained my dilemma

58
00:06:33,330 --> 00:06:39,490
that I had to take leave from
science if I became a programmer.

59
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:47,190
And he said, well, first of all
he pointed out that computers were

60
00:06:47,190 --> 00:06:52,600
here to stay, that it was not a fad.

61
00:06:52,630 --> 00:06:58,534
And then he said he agreed that there
was no such thing as a clear scientific

62
00:06:58,534 --> 00:06:58,551
component in computer programming, but
that I might very well be one of the

63
00:06:58,551 --> 00:06:58,559
people called to make it a science.

64
00:06:58,559 --> 00:06:58,579
And at the time, I was the kind
of guy to whom you could say such

65
00:06:58,579 --> 00:06:58,586
things and would even accept them.

66
00:06:58,586 --> 00:06:58,598
As I said, I was trained
to become a scientist.

67
00:06:58,598 --> 00:06:58,613
When I came in 1952, they were
working on the ARRA, but they

68
00:06:58,613 --> 00:06:58,627
could not get it reliable, and an
updated version, using selenium

69
00:06:58,627 --> 00:06:58,634
diodes instead of relais, was built.

70
00:06:58,634 --> 00:06:58,655
And then the Mathematical Centre built
a machine for Fokker Aircraft Industry,

71
00:06:58,655 --> 00:06:59,422
because before that we had done on the
ARRA lots of calculations for the F27.

72
00:06:59,422 --> 00:06:59,722
So the FERTA, an updated version of the
ARRA, was built and installed at Schiphol.

73
00:06:59,722 --> 00:06:59,747
The installation I did together with
the young Gerard Blaauw who later

74
00:06:59,747 --> 00:06:59,774
became one of the designers of the
IBM 360, with Amdahl and Brooks.

75
00:06:59,774 --> 00:06:59,801
One funny story about the F27, which
Fairchild later built under the name

76
00:06:59,801 --> 00:06:59,828
“Friendship.” On my first visit to
Australia, I flew a big 747 from

77
00:06:59,828 --> 00:06:59,859
Amsterdam to Los Angeles, then I changed
planes and I got on another 747, from

78
00:06:59,859 --> 00:06:59,888
America I flew to Sydney or Melbourne,
I don’t remember which of the two.

79
00:06:59,888 --> 00:06:59,920
And then it was the final part of
the journey on an F27 to Canberra,

80
00:06:59,920 --> 00:06:59,940
because I had been invited by
the Australians to Canberra.

81
00:06:59,940 --> 00:06:59,980
And we arrived and I left the plane and I
met my host, whom I had never met before,

82
00:06:59,980 --> 00:06:59,990
but we had corresponded extensively.

83
00:06:59,990 --> 00:07:00,017
And he was very apologetic that
this world traveler had to do the

84
00:07:00,017 --> 00:07:00,040
last leg of the journey on such
a shaky two-engine turboprop.

85
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:00,071
And it gave me the dear opportunity for
a one-upmanship that I never got again.

86
00:07:00,071 --> 00:07:00,465
I could honestly say, “Dr.

87
00:07:00,465 --> 00:07:00,872
Stanton, I felt quite safe:
I calculated the resonance

88
00:07:00,872 --> 00:07:01,098
frequencies of the wings myself.”

89
00:07:01,098 --> 00:07:01,698
Now was this the same journey that
you continued around the globe?

90
00:07:01,698 --> 00:07:01,916
You stopped in India?

91
00:07:01,916 --> 00:07:03,029
No, no, this was a different
trip, that was the trip to Japan.

92
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:07,913
Okay, in ’54 the completion of the
ARRA, in ’55/’56 I become a programmer.

93
00:07:07,913 --> 00:07:07,926
Then, in 1956, all sorts
of things happened.

94
00:07:07,926 --> 00:08:02,053
I got my degree as a
theoretical physicist.

95
00:08:22,650 --> 00:11:32,419
As soon as I had decided to become a
programmer I finished my studies as

96
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:38,246
quickly as possible, since I no longer
felt welcome at the University: the

97
00:11:38,410 --> 00:11:42,819
physicists would considered me as
a deserter, and the mathematicians,

98
00:11:42,970 --> 00:11:51,860
they were dismissive and somewhat
contemptuous about computing anyhow.

99
00:11:53,420 --> 00:11:54,910
It was all finite, wasn’t it?

100
00:11:57,050 --> 00:11:59,524
In the mathematical culture of those
days in the Netherlands you had

101
00:11:59,524 --> 00:12:01,393
to deal with infinity to make your
topic scientifically respectable.

102
00:12:01,580 --> 00:12:01,991
In 1956 I did two important things,
I got my degree and we had the

103
00:12:01,991 --> 00:12:01,997
festive opening of the ARMAC.

104
00:12:01,997 --> 00:12:02,194
We had to have a demonstration.

105
00:12:02,194 --> 00:12:44,879
Now the ARRA, a few years earlier, had
been so unreliable, that the only safe

106
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:55,439
demonstration we dared to give was the
generation of random numbers, but for the

107
00:12:55,439 --> 00:13:04,170
more reliable ARMAC I could
try something more ambitious.

108
00:13:04,250 --> 00:13:09,360
The problem of course is that for a
demonstration for non-computing people

109
00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:21,300
you have to have a problem statement
that non-mathematicians can understand,

110
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:23,219
even they have to understand the answer.

111
00:13:25,080 --> 00:13:35,910
So I designed a program that would
find the shortest route between two

112
00:13:35,910 --> 00:13:36,385
cities in the Netherlands, using
a somewhat reduced roadmap of the

113
00:13:36,385 --> 00:13:56,449
Netherlands, on which I had selected,
64 cities [so that in the coding, 6

114
00:13:56,479 --> 00:13:59,199
bits would suffice to identify a city].

115
00:13:59,239 --> 00:14:01,659
Somewhat similar to the
seven-bridges problem?

116
00:14:02,639 --> 00:14:06,469
No, it’s just the shortest route.

117
00:14:08,530 --> 00:14:11,247
What’s the shortest way to travel from
Rotterdam to Groningen, in general:

118
00:14:11,247 --> 00:14:11,260
from given city to given city.

119
00:14:11,260 --> 00:14:35,280
It is the algorithm for the shortest path,
which I designed in about twenty minutes.

120
00:14:35,300 --> 00:14:46,345
One morning I was shopping in
Amsterdam with my young fiancée,

121
00:14:46,570 --> 00:14:54,828
and tired, we sat down on the café
terrace to drink a cup of coffee and

122
00:14:54,828 --> 00:14:56,461
I was just thinking about whether I
could do this, and I then designed

123
00:14:56,461 --> 00:14:57,171
the algorithm for the shortest path.

124
00:14:57,290 --> 00:15:15,230
As I said, it was a
twenty-minute invention.

125
00:15:15,550 --> 00:15:19,709
In fact, it was published
in ’59, three years late.

126
00:15:21,750 --> 00:15:25,770
The publication is still readable,
it is, in fact, quite nice.

127
00:15:25,780 --> 00:15:32,510
One of the reasons that it is
so nice was that I designed

128
00:15:32,570 --> 00:15:33,195
it without pencil and paper.

129
00:15:33,195 --> 00:15:33,287
I learned later that one of the
advantages of designing without pencil

130
00:15:33,287 --> 00:15:34,290
and paper is that you are almost forced
to avoid all avoidable complexities.

131
00:15:34,290 --> 00:15:34,353
Eventually that algorithm became,
to my great amazement, one of

132
00:15:34,353 --> 00:15:34,384
the cornerstones of my fame.

133
00:15:34,384 --> 00:16:02,149
I found it in the early ’60’s in a German
book on management science - - “Das

134
00:16:04,409 --> 00:16:07,340
Dijkstra’sche Verfahren.” Suddenly,
there was a method named after me.

135
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:24,289
It’s of economic value.

136
00:16:24,870 --> 00:16:25,500
Yes.

137
00:16:27,210 --> 00:16:36,860
And it jumped again recently because it is
extensively used in all travel planners.

138
00:16:37,860 --> 00:16:44,099
If, these days, you want to go from here
to there and you have a car with a GPS

139
00:16:45,259 --> 00:16:54,069
and a screen, it can give you the shortest
way from your hotel to Robbie Creek Cove.

140
00:16:54,600 --> 00:16:54,880
Yes?

141
00:16:55,140 --> 00:16:59,209
I did generate a map, yes, on line.

142
00:16:59,270 --> 00:17:01,216
Did you use it?

143
00:17:01,216 --> 00:17:01,890
Yes, I did use it.

144
00:17:02,319 --> 00:17:02,652
Then you used my algorithm this morning.

145
00:17:02,710 --> 00:17:16,319
When was that originally published?

146
00:17:17,429 --> 00:17:19,990
It was originally published
in 1959 in a German article in

147
00:17:19,990 --> 00:17:28,139
Numerische Mathematik . 1 F.L.

148
00:17:28,209 --> 00:17:36,120
Bauer had founded that journal and
he was soliciting for articles.

149
00:17:37,969 --> 00:17:45,980
Now, at the time, something like an
algorithm for the shortest path, that was

150
00:17:45,980 --> 00:17:51,900
hardly considered mathematics: there was
a finite number of ways of going from A

151
00:17:51,950 --> 00:18:00,107
to B and obviously there is a shortest
one, so what’s all the fuss about?

152
00:18:00,107 --> 00:18:08,926
So that is why it remained unpublished,
but when Bauer asked whether we could

153
00:18:08,926 --> 00:18:15,220
contribute something, I said, yes, I
could, because in the mean time I had also

154
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:21,850
designed the shortest subspanning tree.

155
00:18:23,190 --> 00:18:25,090
I had done that for my hardware friends.

156
00:18:25,590 --> 00:18:28,740
You know, on the big panel you have
to connect a whole lot of points

157
00:18:28,750 --> 00:18:32,070
with the same copper wire because
they have to have the same voltage.

158
00:18:32,309 --> 00:18:40,357
How do you minimize the amount of
copper wire that connects these points?

159
00:18:40,990 --> 00:18:44,789
So I wrote an article “A note on
two problems in connection with

160
00:18:46,709 --> 00:18:47,290
graphs” or something like that.

161
00:18:53,990 --> 00:19:04,300
But now, when I went to my ophthalmologist
- - and to the best of my knowledge he did not

162
00:19:04,300 --> 00:19:12,580
even know that I was a computing scientist
- - he said, ‘Have you designed the algorithm

163
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:17,582
for GPS?” It turned out he had seen the
Scientific American 2 of last November.

164
00:19:18,610 --> 00:19:19,120
You asked a question.

165
00:19:19,150 --> 00:19:21,480
What did you do with your fame then?

166
00:19:22,300 --> 00:19:23,959
What did I do with my fame?

167
00:19:24,520 --> 00:19:31,280
I was amazed by it!

168
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:36,590
Okay that was ’56.

169
00:19:40,669 --> 00:19:45,670
’57 was the year of my marriage,
which I had to do as a professional

170
00:19:45,670 --> 00:19:48,929
theoretical physicist, crazy story.

171
00:19:52,590 --> 00:19:59,139
Besides that I was confronted
with the real-time interrupt,

172
00:19:59,230 --> 00:20:03,300
and I panicked almost.

173
00:20:04,910 --> 00:20:12,290
Because, you see, for those first
five years I had always been

174
00:20:12,330 --> 00:20:16,699
programming for non-existing
machines, not yet existing machines.

175
00:20:16,770 --> 00:20:24,209
I would discuss, we would design
the instruction code, I would check

176
00:20:24,219 --> 00:20:27,620
whether this was an instruction code
I could live with, and my hardware

177
00:20:27,620 --> 00:20:31,900
friend would check that this was an
instruction code that they could build.

178
00:20:34,350 --> 00:20:39,070
And then I would write down the
formal specification of the machine,

179
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:46,510
and all three of us would sign
it with our blood, so to speak.

180
00:20:47,760 --> 00:20:52,599
And then our ways parted and they
would start, they knew what to build

181
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,280
and I knew what I could construct.

182
00:20:56,589 --> 00:21:00,429
But all the programming
I did was on paper.

183
00:21:03,450 --> 00:21:12,739
So I was quite used to the fact of
developing programs without testing them.

184
00:21:16,110 --> 00:21:17,460
There’s no way to test them?

185
00:21:17,460 --> 00:21:21,180
There was not a way to test them, so
you’ve got to convince yourself of their

186
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:25,080
correctness by reasoning about them.

187
00:21:26,340 --> 00:21:36,429
Now, of course, there could be writing
errors, I never claimed infallibility.

188
00:21:38,249 --> 00:21:42,738
You have to leave that to experts
in that field like the Pope.

189
00:21:42,738 --> 00:22:00,039
But a simple writing error did not matter
as long as the machine wasn’t there yet,

190
00:22:00,039 --> 00:22:04,460
and as soon as errors would show up on the
machine, they would be simple to correct.

191
00:22:05,670 --> 00:22:09,759
However, in ’57, I was confronted
with the idea of the real-time

192
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:17,620
interrupt and that created a vision
of a program with non-reproducible

193
00:22:18,230 --> 00:22:22,709
errors, because a real-time interrupt
occurs at an unpredictable moment.

194
00:22:32,030 --> 00:22:43,990
My hardware friends flattered
me out of my resistance.

195
00:22:45,429 --> 00:22:45,730
They said, “Yes, yes, we see your
problem, but surely you must be up

196
00:22:45,730 --> 00:22:54,090
to it…” I learned to cope with it.

197
00:22:57,510 --> 00:23:03,449
I wrote a real-time interrupt handler
which was flawless and which became

198
00:23:03,450 --> 00:23:03,550
the

199
00:23:03,550 --> 00:23:04,936
topic of my Ph.D.

200
00:23:04,936 --> 00:23:05,283
thesis.

201
00:23:05,629 --> 00:23:18,610
3 Later I would learn that
over here, this would almost be

202
00:23:18,610 --> 00:23:19,980
considered an un-American activity.

203
00:23:24,260 --> 00:23:25,140
Why is that?

204
00:23:28,180 --> 00:23:30,550
Well, the American reaction
was very, very different.

205
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:50,450
When IBM had to develop the software for
the 360, they built one or two machines

206
00:23:50,450 --> 00:23:54,900
especially equipped with a monitor.

207
00:23:56,920 --> 00:24:05,760
That is an extra piece of machinery that
would exactly record when interrupts

208
00:24:06,250 --> 00:24:13,940
took place and from where to where.

209
00:24:13,940 --> 00:24:19,739
And if something went wrong, it
could replay it again and use

210
00:24:19,739 --> 00:24:30,220
the recorded history to control
when interrupts would occur.

211
00:24:30,220 --> 00:24:37,519
So they made it reproducible, yes,
but at the expense of much more

212
00:24:37,519 --> 00:24:40,390
hardware than we could afford.

213
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:45,459
Needless

214
00:24:45,460 --> 00:24:45,540
to

215
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:49,969
say, they never got the OS/360 right.

216
00:24:50,879 --> 00:24:51,310
That’s true.

217
00:24:51,310 --> 00:24:53,000
That’s very true.

218
00:24:54,230 --> 00:24:54,890
Because

219
00:24:58,980 --> 00:25:01,330
…
 Now that would have never
occurred to a European?

220
00:25:02,309 --> 00:25:03,490
No, we were too poor.

221
00:25:04,530 --> 00:25:06,070
To consider it?

222
00:25:06,070 --> 00:25:33,000
We were too poor to consider it and
we also decided that we should try to

223
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:39,080
structure our designs in such a way
that we could keep things under our

224
00:25:39,090 --> 00:25:46,240
intellectual control and that there was
no need to search experimentally for bugs.

225
00:25:47,150 --> 00:26:06,499
This was a major difference between
European and American attitudes about

226
00:26:06,499 --> 00:26:09,139
programming to which I will return later.

227
00:26:13,930 --> 00:26:19,210
Okay, in 1958, I wrote the basic
software and I wrote my thesis about it.

228
00:26:20,290 --> 00:26:28,700
I only had the oral defense a year later
because I wrote my thesis in Dutch.

229
00:26:28,950 --> 00:26:31,300
Those were the days when I could not write
fluently in English, and it was translated

230
00:26:31,350 --> 00:26:38,070
with the aid of a South African colleague.

231
00:26:38,160 --> 00:26:48,720
In 1959, I published those two
algorithms, shortest path and

232
00:26:48,720 --> 00:26:48,903
the shortest subspanning tree.

233
00:26:48,910 --> 00:26:54,030
I had my oral defense and became Dr.

234
00:26:54,030 --> 00:26:54,250
Dijkstra.

235
00:26:56,250 --> 00:27:07,389
And there was one illuminating
experience at the Mathematical Centre.

236
00:27:11,429 --> 00:27:20,760
I had challenged my colleagues with
the following programming task.

237
00:27:22,830 --> 00:27:32,070
Consider two cyclic programs, and
in each cycle occurs a section

238
00:27:32,230 --> 00:27:33,689
called the critical section.

239
00:27:36,100 --> 00:27:41,700
The two programs can
communicate by single reads and

240
00:27:41,800 --> 00:27:42,200
single

241
00:27:42,290 --> 00:27:57,510
writes in a common store, and
about the relative speeds of

242
00:27:58,070 --> 00:27:59,570
the programs nothing is known.

243
00:28:02,860 --> 00:28:06,590
Try to synchronize these programs
in such a way that at any moment

244
00:28:06,610 --> 00:28:14,250
in time at most one of them is
engaged in its critical section.

245
00:28:15,990 --> 00:28:22,511
It’s an implementation of the mutual
exclusion problem which later became

246
00:28:22,511 --> 00:28:34,820
a cornerstone of THE Multiprogramming
System - - underlying the monitor

247
00:28:35,099 --> 00:28:42,340
concept and a whole lot of things.

248
00:28:42,340 --> 00:28:44,800
I looked at it and realized that
it was not trivial at all, there

249
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:46,450
were all sorts of side conditions.

250
00:28:46,469 --> 00:28:50,900
For instance, if

251
00:28:50,900 --> 00:28:56,859
one of the programs would stay for
a very long time in its non-critical

252
00:28:57,099 --> 00:29:03,290
section, the other one should be
able to go on unhampered, so going

253
00:29:03,290 --> 00:29:06,040
critical alternatingly was not allowed.

254
00:29:07,350 --> 00:29:11,820
Another thing that was not allowed was
what we called ‘After-you-after-you’

255
00:29:16,649 --> 00:29:22,179
blocking, where the programs would
compete for access to the critical section

256
00:29:22,520 --> 00:29:24,675
and the dilemma would never be solved.

257
00:29:24,675 --> 00:29:24,768
Now, my friends at the Mathematical
Centre handed in their solutions,

258
00:29:24,768 --> 00:29:24,811
but they were all wrong.

259
00:29:24,820 --> 00:29:55,960
For each purported solution I would sketch
a scenario that would reveal the bug.

260
00:29:56,250 --> 00:30:00,830
Well, what happened then was that
people made their programs more

261
00:30:01,090 --> 00:30:05,410
sophisticated, more complicated,
and one or two days later they would

262
00:30:05,460 --> 00:30:06,870
come down with their next version.

263
00:30:07,320 --> 00:30:13,499
It was wrong as well, only the
construction and counter-examples

264
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:17,070
became even more time-consuming and I
had to change the rules of the game.

265
00:30:17,949 --> 00:30:24,060
I said, “Sir, sorry but I can
no longer spend all my days

266
00:30:24,190 --> 00:30:26,690
refuting your vain efforts.

267
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:37,390
From now onwards I only accept a solution
with an argument why it is correct.”

268
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:42,640
With the program correctness?

269
00:30:42,650 --> 00:30:47,556
Yes, and within three hours or so Th.

270
00:30:47,556 --> 00:30:48,273
J.

271
00:30:48,990 --> 00:30:57,750
Dekker came with a perfect solution
and a proof of its correctness.

272
00:30:57,750 --> 00:31:09,079
He had analyzed what kind
of proof would be needed.

273
00:31:09,139 --> 00:31:10,860
What are the things I have to show?

274
00:31:10,930 --> 00:31:12,509
How can I prove them?

275
00:31:13,219 --> 00:31:18,080
What would be an acceptable
structure of the correctness proof?

276
00:31:19,080 --> 00:31:27,249
Having settled that, he wrote down a
program that met the proof’s requirement.

277
00:31:32,310 --> 00:31:42,489
Which very clearly shows that you
lose a lot when you restrict the

278
00:31:42,489 --> 00:31:45,053
role of mathematics to program
verification as opposed to program

279
00:31:45,053 --> 00:31:45,822
construction or derivation.

280
00:31:45,822 --> 00:32:17,909
That was what we learned from the
competition that was won by Dekker.

281
00:32:17,909 --> 00:32:33,159
[Excuse me for a moment.]

282
00:32:41,570 --> 00:32:52,810
. . . . .
Another experience of 1959 was the Zeroth
IFIP Congress in Paris, a precursor

283
00:32:52,860 --> 00:32:57,300
under the auspices of the United Nations.

284
00:33:03,360 --> 00:33:12,150
I attended that, though I was still
a not too well-known youngster.

285
00:33:12,150 --> 00:33:23,092
I had visited England a few times,
I had visited Germany a few times.

286
00:33:23,420 --> 00:33:33,130
My international contacts had
started in December ’58, when

287
00:33:38,440 --> 00:33:46,240
the preparatory meetings for
the design of ALGOL 60 started.

288
00:33:51,470 --> 00:33:56,279
Normally the Mathematical Centre
would have been represented by my

289
00:33:56,279 --> 00:34:02,483
boss, Aad van Wijingaarden, but he had
had a serious car accident, and J.A.

290
00:34:02,949 --> 00:34:20,290
Zonneveld and I, as his immediate
underlings, had to replace him.

291
00:34:20,440 --> 00:34:26,629
Zonneveld was a numerical analyst and
did the numerical work, while I, as a

292
00:34:26,629 --> 00:34:28,449
programmer, did the programming work.

293
00:34:28,869 --> 00:34:41,349
And so I began attending the
preparatory meetings for ALGOL 60.

294
00:34:41,949 --> 00:34:50,740
That was about the first time that
I had to carry out spontaneously

295
00:34:50,740 --> 00:34:53,309
discussions in English.

296
00:34:54,479 --> 00:34:55,539
It was tough.

297
00:34:56,050 --> 00:34:56,760
How did you learn?

298
00:35:01,160 --> 00:35:07,330
Well, I had, of course, had
English in school, in high school.

299
00:35:10,190 --> 00:35:17,290
I had read a lot, I had no
experience in writing or speaking it.

300
00:35:21,120 --> 00:35:26,420
I learned an awful lot from
friends as soon as they knew

301
00:35:26,470 --> 00:35:28,160
that I liked to be corrected.

302
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:38,940
And I’m extremely grateful to Mike
Woodger of the National Physical

303
00:35:38,940 --> 00:35:43,670
Laboratory, Teddington, whom I met
in December ’58 for the first time.

304
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:52,020
He quickly made it a rule for himself
that he would only correct those

305
00:35:52,059 --> 00:35:55,258
errors he heard me make the second
time, because otherwise conversation

306
00:35:55,258 --> 00:35:55,616
would have been impossible.

307
00:35:55,616 --> 00:36:13,140
That greatly helped and I think that
after three or four years or so, I

308
00:36:13,140 --> 00:36:13,745
was beginning to feel comfortable.

309
00:36:13,889 --> 00:36:22,270
I heard you remark that learning
many different languages

310
00:36:22,310 --> 00:36:23,576
is useful to programming.

311
00:36:23,576 --> 00:36:28,850
Oh yes, it’s useful.

312
00:36:33,410 --> 00:36:40,600
There is an enormous difference between
one who is monolingual and someone

313
00:36:41,040 --> 00:36:48,469
who at least knows a second language
well, because it makes you much

314
00:36:48,469 --> 00:36:59,450
